in Malang this is a community in  mourning and shock seventeen-year-old  wedad went to a football match his  family never expected that he wouldn't  make it home  the game turned to tragedy after police  fired tear gas at Spectators apparently  to control fans who entered the field  after the match ended  people panicked triggering a stampede as  they rushed to the gates  Rehan zelani survived but his friend  didn't  you know police through tear gas to the  lower part of the stadium I saw children  dying in front of my eyes people were  scattering around in panic trying to  escape but the exits were locked so they  were piling up their hundreds were  injured in the chaos Witnesses paint a  horrific picture  as people ran towards the exit it was  locked and then the lights dimmed in the  stadium it was dark people were pushing  each other and even stepping over each  other trying to get out this is the  waiting area of the saiful Anwar  Hospital in Malang one of the hospitals  close to the stadium most of the people  waiting here are parents waiting  anxiously for updates on their children  seventeen-year-old rafana DUI afrancia  is in the Intensive Care Unit  I was staying up waiting for him it was  so late and he hadn't come home everyone  else in our neighborhood who had been to  the match had started to come home where  was my son  as she worries about her son she Ponders  why he ended up in hospital in the first  place  why did police gas them there were many  children up there and mothers too now  you see who the victims are many of them  are just young boys like my son  putri's mother is also waiting for an  update from the ICU  her face was swollen and the bones in  her neck dislocated she's unconscious  Indonesia's president has asked for an  investigation and human rights groups  are calling for answers and  accountability criticizing police for  using tear gas in a confined space  for now many of the parents who spoke to  Al Jazeera say the only question on  their mind is whether their child will  make it through Jessica Washington Al  Jazeera Malang
 

